p4. two law families, two consumer classes, exhaustive at 4 bits
============================================================================

Per cell. 'mono' is violations of monotonicity over the reachable set for
addition; 'trop' is violations of + distributing over min; 'add' and 'mul'
are violations of associativity of the induced operation. All over Q^3.

window           policy                   mono    trop     add     mul
----------------------------------------------------------------------
unsigned [0,15]  wrap                      120    1360       0       0
unsigned [0,15]  saturate                    0       0       0       0
unsigned [0,15]  opposite-bound mutant     225    1360    2240       0
signed [-8,7]    wrap                      232    1360       0       0
signed [-8,7]    saturate                    0       0     952     160
signed [-8,7]    opposite-bound mutant     281    1360    1386     604

min is closed on Q in every cell (adaptations needed for min over Q^2): wrap/unsi=0, satu/unsi=0, oppo/unsi=0, wrap/sign=0, satu/sign=0, oppo/sign=0

============================================================================
THE DERIVED CLAIM, checked rather than asserted:
  tropical distributivity holds  <==>  the reduction is monotone

  biconditional over all 6 cells: True
    unsigned [0,15]  wrap                   monotone=False distributes=False
    unsigned [0,15]  saturate               monotone=True  distributes=True 
    unsigned [0,15]  opposite-bound mutant  monotone=False distributes=False
    signed [-8,7]    wrap                   monotone=False distributes=False
    signed [-8,7]    saturate               monotone=True  distributes=True 
    signed [-8,7]    opposite-bound mutant  monotone=False distributes=False

  and the two hypotheses observed both true and false across the cells, so
  the biconditional is not holding by accident:
    monotone true in 2 of 6 cells, false in 4

============================================================================
THE TWO CONSUMERS, per cell.
  TROPICAL consumer  (shortest path, min-plus, widest-path, any semiring
                      computation whose additive operation is the order):
                      needs the ADAPTATION laws. Coherence is irrelevant.
  REASSOCIATING fold (splitting, threading, algebraic rewriting):
                      needs COHERENCE. Monotonicity is irrelevant.

window           policy                  tropical ok   fold ok   both
---------------------------------------------------------------------
unsigned [0,15]  wrap                          False      True  False
unsigned [0,15]  saturate                       True      True   True
unsigned [0,15]  opposite-bound mutant         False     False  False
signed [-8,7]    wrap                          False      True  False
signed [-8,7]    saturate                       True     False  False
signed [-8,7]    opposite-bound mutant         False     False  False

  cells serving BOTH consumers for addition: 1 of 6
    unsigned [0,15], saturate

  This reproduces `63` section 3.4's inhabited two-by-two from the consumer
  side: the cell holding both law families is unsigned saturation over a
  nonnegative window, and that is exactly the workload shape of nonnegative
  shortest path. The default the imitation intent points at (I3, signed) is
  in a cell that serves one consumer or the other and never both.

  What this does not establish: anything about multiplication under the
  tropical reading (the tropical product is the numeral's add, already
  covered by the 'add' column); anything at nonzero fraction width; any
  magnitude whatsoever. One model width, exhaustive within it.
